      "knowledge_point": "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity: Case With Alternating Signs",
      "problem": "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.94)^{5}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}≈1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.06$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.94)^{6} & =(1+(-0.06))^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(-0.06)+10(-0.06)^{2} \\\\  & =1-5(0.06)+10(0.003\\,6) \\\\  & =1-0.3+0.036 \\\\  & =0.736,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places.",
