      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Percentage Error of an Approximation",
      "problem": "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(2.92)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(243)+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.08$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.92)^{5} & =(3+(-0.08))^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(-0.08)+270(-0.08)^{2} \\\\  & =243-405(0.08)+270(0.006\\,4) \\\\  & =243-32.4+1.728 \\\\  & =212.328\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(2.92)^{5}-212.328|}{(2.92)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
